      {
        "id": "p42-q21a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the two intersection points of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ with the line $y=4-3x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\frac13,3\\right)$ and $(1,1)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1x&=4-3x\\\\3x^2-4x+1&=0\\\\(3x-1)(x-1)&=0\\\\x&=\\frac13\\text{ or }x=1.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into $y=\\frac1x$, the intersection points are $\\left(\\frac13,3\\right)$ and $(1,1)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q21b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the area between these two curves.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\frac43-\\ln3\\right)\\text{ units}^2$.",
          "worked": "On $\\left[\\frac13,1\\right]$, the line $y=4-3x$ is above $y=\\frac1x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=\\int_{1/3}^{1}\\left((4-3x)-\\frac1x\\right)\\,dx\\\\&=\\left[4x-\\frac{3x^2}{2}-\\ln x\\right]_{1/3}^{1}\\\\&=\\left(4-\\frac32\\right)-\\left(\\frac43-\\frac16-\\ln\\frac13\\right)\\\\&=\\frac52-\\left(\\frac76+\\ln3\\right)\\\\&=\\left(\\frac43-\\ln3\\right)\\text{ units}^2.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      }
    ]
  },
